.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_TLS 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_tls \- set the TLS configuration of a client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_tls(chttpcli cli,
                                  const chttp_tls_config_t *tls);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function copies the TLS configuration that you give into the
client. A tls of NULL puts the defaults back: verification of the
chain and of the hostname, the system CA bundle and no client
certificate.

The function deep-copies the strings of chttp_tls_config_t (cert_path,
key_path and ca_bundle_path) into buffers that the client owns, so the
caller can free or reuse its own originals at once after this call
returns.

This function does not check that it can read cert_path, key_path and
ca_bundle_path; the client checks each path later, at the moment an
HTTPS request needs it. A path that points at no file at all, or at a
directory or another object that is not a file, is accepted here with
no error, and then gives ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed from
chttpclient_do or from chttpclient_do_streaming once a request needs
it.

The client reads each of the three files whole, and rejects a file
above 16 MB with ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed without reading it
into memory. A PEM artefact is much smaller than that (a certificate
or a key is a few kilobytes, and a full system CA bundle is a few
hundred kilobytes), so only a path that points at something other than
a PEM file reaches the limit.

ca_bundle_path must also give at least one certificate. A file that
the client can read can still parse to no certificate: an empty file,
a key on its own, or a file that is not PEM at all. The client reports
ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed for such files, because it does not
accept a trust store that trusts no issuer.

ca_bundle_path may also hold certificate revocation lists (CRLs)
beside its CA certificates. The client then enforces them against the
certificate chain of every server, as the ssl_crl directive of nginx
does: every certificate of that chain below its trust anchor needs a
CRL of its issuer in the bundle. A request fails with
ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed for a revoked certificate, for a
certificate whose issuer has no CRL in the bundle, and for a CRL past
its next update time. A bundle with no CRL checks no revocation.

cert_path and key_path are a pair. If you set one of the two and leave
the other NULL, the function gives ccol_invalid_args instead of
treating that state as "no client certificate", because such silence
leaves an mTLS deployment believing that it presents a client
certificate while it never does.

A chttp_tls_config_t whose every byte is zero is the SAFE
configuration: it verifies the certificate chain of the server and
matches its hostname. Both verification fields are named for what
turning them ON gives up, so a struct built with = {0}, or with a
designated initializer that names only some of the fields, can never
end up with less verification than the caller asked for.

insecure_skip_verify accepts any certificate from any peer, and also
makes insecure_skip_hostname_check irrelevant: a hostname matched
against a certificate that nothing validated proves nothing, so there
is no way to ask for the hostname check without the chain check.

insecure_skip_hostname_check keeps the chain verification and drops
only the hostname match. See CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3) for more.

The new configuration applies to every request after this call
returns, including a request that could reuse a keep-alive connection
from the pool. The key of both idle pools is the origin
("scheme://host:port", or "unix://<path>"), and that key says nothing
about the TLS policy of the handshake of a pooled connection. The
client therefore retires, instead of reusing, every pooled HTTPS
connection that it made under the configuration that this call
replaces, and the next request to that origin does a fresh handshake
under the new settings. The client keeps reusing a pooled plain-HTTP
connection, because no TLS setting applies to it. A connection that
another request already holds out of the pool finishes under the
configuration that it started with: the client retires only the
connections that are still idle.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli   The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
tls   The TLS configuration to copy, or NULL to put the defaults
      back.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success          The client has the new configuration.
ccol_invalid_args     cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale handle of
                      a client that is already destroyed, or you set
                      exactly one of tls->cert_path and
                      tls->key_path, or you set
                      tls->insecure_skip_verify together with a
                      tls->ca_bundle_path. A bundle exists to be
                      verified against, so that pair states two
                      incompatible policies, and the function
                      refuses it rather than picking one.
ccol_not_enough_memory
                      An allocation of memory failed. The TLS
                      configuration of the client then holds the
                      defaults (verification of the chain and of
                      the hostname, the system CA bundle and no
                      client certificate); the client does not
                      apply part of your configuration.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT (3),
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3)
